{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"6edbdee9-eb41-4035-ba5e-483a55e1dafe","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now the current TS number-literal (after fix):\n\nFrom earlier read of current file lines 400-410:\n```clojure\n  [n text]\n  (let [classified (numeric-token/classify :typescript text)]\n    (case (:kind classified)\n      :float64 (u/lit (numeric-token/materialize-classified classified))\n      :invalid (syntax! n (if (= :legacy-octal (:reason classified))\n                            \"Legacy octal literals are not allowed in strict mode\"\n                            \"Invalid or unexpected token\")))))\n```\n\nSo the new classify-typescript:\n- strips separators\n- `0x` → digits `[0-9a-f]+` (lowercased) → :float64 radix 16\n- `0o` → `[0-7]+` → :float64 radix 8\n- `0b` → `[01]+` → :float64 radix 2\n- `^0[0-9]` → :invalid legacy-octal\n- else → :float64 text\n\nCompare with old number-literal:\n- strip `_`\n- lowercase\n- `0x` → radix-double (validates `[0-9a-fA-F]+`)\n- `0o` → radix-double `[0-7]+`\n- `0b` → radix-double `[01]+`\n- `^0[0-9]` → syntax! \"Legacy octal...\"\n- else → host-double (decimal-text t)\n\nThese are equivalent. The new version lowercases in classify-typescript (`str/lower-case t`), and digits checks use `[0-9a-f]+` etc. (lowercase). The radix-double validation in old used case-insensitive `[0-9a-fA-F]+`. Same.\n\nOne subtle difference: old `radix-double` used `(double radix)` for the radix in accumulation and `str/lower-case` on each char for index. New `parse-radix-float64` uses `(double radix)` and `(str/lower-case (str ch))`. Same.\n\nAnother subtle: old `decimal-text` did NOT strip leading `+` — it only handles leading `.` and trailing `.`. But wait, TS Number literals don't have leading `+` (that's the unary operator, not part of the literal token). The `:typescript` dialect sets `:allow-signed? false`. So no `+`/`-` handling. The `normalize-decimal-float` in parse-float64 strips leading `+` if present, but for TS there's no `+` in the token. For the `-` case — negative numbers in TS/JS are `-` unary minus applied to a positive literal, so the literal token itself is unsigned. But what about `-0.0`? In JS that's `-` unary on `0.0`. The literal token is `0.0`. OK.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtle difference. Old `host-double` (parse) did NOT handle leading `+` either, but it did handle the leading-dot via `decimal-text` (`0.` → `00.`? no: `(str \"0\" t)` → `0.5` → `00.5`? No: `(str/starts-with? t \".\")` → t = \"0\" + \".5\" = \"0.5\". Good). And trailing dot: `2.` → `2.0`. Good.\n\nNew `normalize-decimal-float` does: leading `.` → `0.`, `.e`/`.E` → `.0e`, trailing `.` → `.0`. Same as `decimal-text`, plus leading `+` strip and leading `-` preserve. Since TS tokens are unsigned, the `+`/`-` handling is a no-op for TS. Fine.\n\nActually wait, there's a subtle issue: `normalize-decimal-float` — the `.e` replacement happens AFTER leading-dot handling but the order in old `decimal-text` was: leading dot first, then `.e`/`.E`, then trailing dot. New: leading dot, then `.e`, then `.E`, then trailing dot. Same order. Good.\n\nSo TS behavior is preserved. But there's one more subtlety: old TS `number-literal` did `(str/replace text \"_\" \"\")` FIRST, then checked `0x` prefix. New classify-typescript strips separator first too (`strip-separator`). Same.\n\nAlso, hex float with `p` exponent: old would `subs lower 2` → e.g. `0x1.8p1` → `1.8p1`, then `radix-double` validates `[0-9a-fA-F]+` which FAILS (because `.` and `p`). So old code would `syntax!` error on `0x1.8p1`. New code: `re-matches #\"[0-9a-f]+\"` on `1.8p1` fails → `:invalid` → syntax! error. Same behavior (both refuse hex float with exponent). Actually wait — does JS even allow `0x1.8p1`? Yes, JS allows hex float literals with `p` exponent. But the ANTLR grammar and the pre-fix lowerer already refused them. So the new code preserves the pre-fix (possibly limited) behavior. That's what \"no change in golden addresses\" means — the control is preserved. OK, this is consistent with \"retain their semantics (their suites as controls)\".\n\nNow let me check Python and PHP diffs.","signature":"6edbdee9-eb41-4035-ba5e-483a55e1dafe"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":791,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":63360,"output_tokens":0,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9f2c1e4a-7b3d-4e8f-a6c5-d0e9f8a1b2c3","uuid":"6371bb75-3a66-43e3-94dd-8690848b75be","timestamp":"2026-11-26T15:27:50.988Z","thinking_duration_ms":16750,"thinking_display":"updates"}
