`j` check remains. Good.\n\nBut there's a subtle difference for `float64-token :python`: `parse-float64 (strip-separator :python text)`. `parse-float64` calls `normalize-decimal-float`. The old code did `(Double/parseDouble t)` where `t` = text with `_` stripped, WITHOUT the leading-dot/trailing-dot normalization. Wait — the old Python code did NOT apply `decimal-text`-style normalization! Let me check: old Python `parse-number` float branch:\n```clojure\n(let [t (str/replace text \"_\" \"\")]\n  (literal ctx :float (cbor/float64 (Double/parseDouble t))))\n```\n\nNo leading-dot/trailing-dot normalization. But Python float literals like `.5` and `2.` — does `Double/parseDouble` accept `.5`? Yes, `Double.parseDouble(\".5\")` = 0.5. And `2.`? `Double.parseDouble(\"2.\")` = 2.0. So the old code relied on `Double/parseDouble`/`js/parseFloat`/`double/parse` accepting those forms.\n\nNew `parse-float64` → `normalize-decimal-float` → adds leading `0` to `.5` → `0.5`, and `2.` → `2.0`. The result is the same double value. And for `:cljd` (Dart) `double/parse` — does Dart's double.parse accept `.5`? The old code relied on it. New normalizes first. Either way same value.\n\nActually wait — is there a behavioral difference for the `:cljd` case? Old: `(double/parse \".5\")` — Dart's `double.parse` might actually reject `.5`? Hmm. But the new normalize makes it `0.5` which Dart accepts. This could be a subtle IMPROVEMENT or behavior-preserving. Not a regression concern for the review since Python suites pass.\n\nActually, more importantly: the old Python code `(re-find #\"[.e]\" lower)` — note this matches `.` or `e` ANYWHERE, including in the exponent. Same in new classify. Identical.\n\nOne more: old Python `parse-number` float detection used `(str/ends-with? lower \"j\")` check FIRST. But there's also the `int-literal` for the `:integer` branch. The new classify-python returns `:integer` for non-float (including things like `0x1A`). But note: the old float-detection excluded `0x`, `0o`, `0b` prefixes. New classify-python: `(not (re-find #\"^0[xob]\" lower))`. Same.\n\nBut wait — what about a Python integer like `0x1e5`? Lowercase `0x1e5`. `(not (re-find #\"^0[xob]\" ...))` = false (starts with `0x`), so → `:integer`. Then int-literal handles hex. Good.\n\nWhat about `1_000`? Old: strip `_` → `1000`, `(re-find #\"[.e]\")` false → int-literal path. New: classify → `:integer` → int-literal. Same.\n\nOK Python/PHP/TS adaptations look behavior-preserving. Good.\n\nPHP: old float-literal stripped `_` and parsed. New `float64-token :php` → `parse-float64 (strip-separator :php text)`. Note: old did NOT do leading-dot/trailing-dot normalization; new `parse-float64` normalizes. PHP float tokens like `.5` and `2.` — old relied on parseDouble. New normalizes. Same value. And PHP grammar: does the float-literal ever get leading `+`? PHP allows `+1.0`? The `:php` dialect has `:allow-signed? true`. normalize-decimal-float strips leading `+`. Old code: `(Double/parseDouble \"+1.0\")` — accepts `+1.0` fine. So same. But wait — does the old PHP code strip leading `+`? It didn't, but parseDouble accepts `+1.0`. New normalizes to `1.0`. Same value. Good.\n\nActually, there's a subtle: PHP dialect `:allow-signed? true`, but classify-php doesn't actually validate sign — it just returns `:float64 :text (strip-separator)`. And float64-token → parse-float64. For `-1.0`, parse-float64 preserves `-`. Good.\n\nSo the three adapters retain semantics. Good. Now I should verify the PHP producer test added and the fact that PHP's `Real` token lowercase includes the decimal. This is fine.\n\nNow the important question: the shared factory's `:clojure` dialect `materialize` for the reader. Let me verify the reader uses `materialize :clojure` (yes, line 520 for ##, line 654 for numbers). Good.\n\nNow let me examine yang/clojure.cljc (carrier admission, read-forms wiring), yang/clojure/numeric.cljc (primitives), yin/repl.cljc and frontends.cljc (routing + revision), and the