{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_00_ET_0416gZuFQrnxofxSUjQJ5530","type":"tool_result","content":"25:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_11\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,245p' ../datomworld/src/jolt/dao/host/hash.jolt; sed -n '1,220p' ../datomworld/src/jolt/dao/host/text.jolt; rg -n 'jolt|Gambit' ../datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/main.cljc | tail -35\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"(ns dao.host.hash\\n  \\\"The jolt override of src/cljc/dao/host/hash.cljc (the :jolt alias puts\\n   src/jolt first, docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md §4.2, §6.2,\\n   §6.3): the same public entries, arities, docstrings and refusal data\\n   (docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md §4.2, table A).\\n\\n   P entries (§2.4): `blake3-bytes` and `sha256-bytes`. They take host\\n   bytes (jolt's byte array) and return fresh host bytes representing the\\n   32-byte digest (256 bits). They leave their arguments unmodified.\\n\\n   Both are `:blocking` calls into native/jolt-crypto, which `bb\\n   build:jolt-native` builds: BLAKE3 is the vendored upstream C reference,\\n   SHA-256 the declared system libcrypto's EVP API. The input is copied into\\n   caller-owned native memory (never a C string, so embedded NUL is data),\\n   the digest is read back into a fresh jolt byte array, and the memory is\\n   freed on every path: each native acquisition owns its own release,\\n   established as soon as it is acquired (`with-native`), so a later\\n   allocation or copy that fails still frees every earlier buffer, and each\\n   free runs whether or not another failed.\\n\\n   Loading this namespace is the provenance gate (§6.3): it refuses, by\\n   throwing, unless the wrapper writes this namespace's layout and reports\\n   that every EVP entry resolved to exactly the library jolt-toolchain.edn\\n   qualified for this platform (:native :libcrypto :qualified): the same\\n   canonical real path (which the declared :path must also resolve to), the\\n   same OpenSSL_version_num and the same file SHA-256, at no lower than\\n   :min-version. An upgrade, a repointed link or a replaced file therefore\\n   fails until requalified (the procedure is in jolt-toolchain.edn). No\\n   digest is computed before that check passes; a missing library fails the\\n   load.\\n\\n   String digesting is a table B portable composition over `dao.host.text`\\n   and these byte primitives (`blake3`, `sha256`, `digest-string`,\\n   `digest-bytes`), hosted in `dao.jing`.\\\"\\n  (:require\\n    [clojure.edn :as edn]\\n    [clojure.string :as str]\\n    [dao.host.bytes :as bytes]\\n    [jolt.ffi :as ffi]))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; The native library and its provenance\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(def ^:private platform\\n  ;; as yang.antlr.cst decides it: jolt's os.name/os.arch shim can answer\\n  ;; nil, which reads as Linux and x86_64\\n  (let [os (str/lower-case (or (System/getProperty \\\"os.name\\\") \\\"\\\"))\\n        arch (System/getProperty \\\"os.arch\\\")]\\n    (str (if (str/includes? os \\\"mac\\\") \\\"darwin\\\" \\\"linux\\\") \\\"-\\\"\\n         (if (#{\\\"aarch64\\\" \\\"arm64\\\"} arch) \\\"arm64\\\" \\\"x86_64\\\"))))\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private library\\n  ;; the wrapper `bb build:jolt-native` builds, under the working directory\\n  (str (System/getProperty \\\"user.dir\\\") \\\"/build/native/\\\" platform\\n       \\\"/jolt-crypto/lib/libjolt_crypto\\\"\\n       (if (str/starts-with? platform \\\"darwin\\\") \\\".dylib\\\" \\\".so\\\")))\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private libcrypto-declaration\\n  (get-in (edn/read-string (slurp (str (System/getProperty \\\"user.dir\\\")\\n                                       \\\"/jolt-toolchain.edn\\\")))\\n          [:native :libcrypto]))\\n\\n\\n(ffi/load-library library)\\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-layout-version \\\"jolt_crypto_layout_version\\\" [] :int)\\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-provenance \\\"jolt_crypto_provenance\\\" [:pointer :size_t] :int)\\n\\n\\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-blake3 \\\"jolt_crypto_blake3\\\"\\n            [:pointer :size_t :pointer] :int :blocking)\\n\\n\\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-sha256 \\\"jolt_crypto_sha256\\\"\\n            [:pointer :size_t :pointer] :int :blocking)\\n\\n\\n;; 3 (J2): the random entry joined the exported set (jolt_crypto_random for\\n;; yin.vm.linker.sign/generate's :jolt arm); a J1b-built wrapper answers 2 and\\n;; is refused here rather than at a missing-symbol error in the linker.\\n;; 4 (J6c): jolt_crypto_sha1 joined it (dao.stream.ws.crypto) and EVP_sha1\\n;; the provenance audit; a J2-built wrapper answers 3 and is refused here.\\n(def ^:private layout 4)\\n\\n\\n(defn- refuse-load\\n  [message data]\\n  (throw (ex-info (str \\\"jolt-crypto refused: \\\" message)\\n                  (assoc data :library library))))\\n\\n\\n(when-not (= layout (c-layout-version))\\n  (refuse-load \\\"the library writes another layout\\\"\\n               {:expected layout, :actual (c-layout-version)}))\\n\\n\\n(defn- with-native\\n  \\\"(f p) over caller-owned native memory `p` of `n` bytes (one for an empty\\n   input), freed however `f` exits. The release is established before\\n   anything else can fail, so nesting one call per acquisition frees each\\n   buffer independently of the others.\\\"\\n  [n f]\\n  (let [p (ffi/alloc (max 1 n))]\\n    (try\\n      (when (ffi/null? p)\\n        (throw (ex-info \\\"jolt-crypto native allocation failed\\\" {:bytes n})))\\n      (f p)\\n      (finally (ffi/free p)))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- version-number\\n  \\\"OpenSSL_version_num of a \\\\\\\"major.minor.patch\\\\\\\" text (OpenSSL 3's\\n   0xMNN00PP0 form).\\\"\\n  [text]\\n  (let [[major minor patch] (map #(Long/parseLong %) (str/split text #\\\"\\\\.\\\"))]\\n    (+ (bit-shift-left major 28) (bit-shift-left minor 20) (bit-shift-left patch 4))))\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private provenance\\n  (with-native 4096\\n    (fn [buf]\\n      (let [n (c-provenance buf 4096)]\\n        (when (neg? n)\\n          (refuse-load \\\"libcrypto provenance unavailable\\\" {:status n}))\\n        (let [[num text path digest] (str/split (ffi/read-bytes buf n) #\\\"\\\\t\\\")]\\n          {:version-num (Long/parseLong num), :version text, :path path,\\n           :sha256 digest})))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- canonical\\n  [path]\\n  (.getCanonicalPath (java.io.File. ^String path)))\\n\\n\\n(let [declared (get-in libcrypto-declaration [:path (keyword platform)])\\n      qualified (get-in libcrypto-declaration [:qualified (keyword platform)])\\n      minimum (:min-version libcrypto-declaration)\\n      refuse (fn [message]\\n               (refuse-load message {:declared declared, :qualified qualified,\\n                                     :provenance provenance}))]\\n  (when-not (and declared qualified minimum)\\n    (refuse \\\"jolt-toolchain.edn qualifies no libcrypto for this platform\\\"))\\n  ;; canonical paths: dyld answers the real path behind Homebrew's opt/\\n  ;; link, and a Linux loader may name the file through /lib\\n  (when-not (= (canonical (:path provenance)) (:canonical-path qualified))\\n    (refuse \\\"EVP resolved to an undeclared libcrypto (canonical path differs from the qualified one)\\\"))\\n  (when-not (= (canonical declared) (:canonical-path qualified))\\n    (refuse \\\"the declared libcrypto path resolves to another file than the qualified one (repointed)\\\"))\\n  (when-not (= (:version-num provenance) (:version-num qualified))\\n    (refuse \\\"libcrypto version differs from the qualified one (upgrade or downgrade)\\\"))\\n  (when-not (= (:sha256 provenance) (:sha256 qualified))\\n    (refuse \\\"libcrypto file digest differs from the qualified one (replaced)\\\"))\\n  (when (< (:version-num provenance) (version-number minimum))\\n    (refuse \\\"libcrypto is older than the declared minimum\\\")))\\n\\n\\n(defn- digest\\n  \\\"The 32-byte result of wrapper entry `c-fn` over `bs`, copied into a fresh\\n   jolt byte array.\\\"\\n  [entry c-fn bs]\\n  (let [n (alength bs)]\\n    (with-native n\\n      (fn [in]\\n        (when (pos? n) (ffi/write-array in bs))\\n        (with-native 32\\n          (fn [out]\\n            (let [status (c-fn in n out)]\\n              (when-not (zero? status)\\n                (throw (ex-info (str \\\"jolt-crypto \\\" entry \\\" failed\\\")\\n                                {:entry entry, :status status})))\\n              (ffi/read-array out 32))))))))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; The profile surface\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(defn- check-bytes!\\n  [entry bs]\\n  (when-not (bytes/bytes? bs)\\n    (throw (ex-info (str \\\"dao.host refused: \\\" entry \\\" unsupported-value (expected host bytes)\\\")\\n                    {:dao.host/refusal :unsupported-value\\n                     :dao.host/entry entry}))))\\n\\n\\n(defn blake3-bytes\\n  \\\"P. 32-byte BLAKE3 digest of host bytes `bs`, returning fresh host bytes.\\\"\\n  [bs]\\n  (check-bytes! 'blake3-bytes bs)\\n  (digest 'blake3-bytes c-blake3 bs))\\n\\n\\n(defn sha256-bytes\\n  \\\"P. 32-byte SHA-256 digest of host bytes `bs`, returning fresh host bytes.\\\"\\n  [bs]\\n  (check-bytes! 'sha256-bytes bs)\\n  (digest 'sha256-bytes c-sha256 bs))\\n(ns dao.host.text\\n  \\\"The jolt override of src/cljc/dao/host/text.cljc (the :jolt alias puts\\n   src/jolt first, docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md §4.2): the\\n   same public entries, arities and refusal data\\n   (docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md §4.2, table A).\\n\\n   A jolt string is a Chez string: a sequence of Unicode scalar values\\n   (code points), not of UTF-16 code units; `(count \\\\\\\"😀\\\\\\\")` is 1, and no\\n   Chez string can hold a surrogate code point (V: `integer->char` refuses\\n   #xD800). The code-unit entries are therefore computed over the UTF-16\\n   view of the scalar values: `unit-count` counts a code point above\\n   U+FFFF as two units and `code-unit` answers its high or low surrogate.\\n   Exactly the strings with every surrogate paired are representable, so:\\n\\n   - `units->string` builds every string the profile admits EXCEPT one\\n     holding an unpaired surrogate, which a Chez string cannot hold. It\\n     does not invent a value for that input: it throws an ex-info whose\\n     data carries `:jolt/unimplemented` (and no `:dao.host/refusal`). J1\\n     stopped on that entry; the options are in the J1 report.\\n   - `utf8-encode`'s unpaired-surrogate refusal can never be reached on\\n     jolt, because no jolt string has an unpaired surrogate; the check is\\n     kept, verbatim, over the code-unit view.\\n   - `utf8-decode` is a strict RFC 3629 decoder written here over the\\n     bytes (Unicode table 3-7), never Chez's `utf8->string`, which\\n     substitutes; a leading BOM is content.\\n   - `nfc` is Chez's `string-normalize-nfc`. Chez 10.4 implements Unicode\\n     17.0.0 (release notes, \\\\\\\"Unicode 17.0.0 support (10.4.0)\\\\\\\"), not the\\n     profile's pinned 16.0; by the Unicode normalization stability policy\\n     the two agree on every string of 16.0-assigned code points, and may\\n     differ only on code points first assigned in 17.0.\\\"\\n  (:require\\n    [jolt.scheme :as scheme]))\\n\\n\\n(defn- refuse\\n  [entry cls detail]\\n  (throw (ex-info (str \\\"dao.host refused: \\\" entry \\\" \\\" (name cls) \\\" (\\\" detail \\\")\\\")\\n                  {:dao.host/refusal cls :dao.host/entry entry :detail detail})))\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private chez-string-length (scheme/proc \\\"string-length\\\"))\\n(def ^:private chez-string-ref (scheme/proc \\\"string-ref\\\"))\\n(def ^:private chez-char->integer (scheme/proc \\\"char->integer\\\"))\\n(def ^:private chez-integer->char (scheme/proc \\\"integer->char\\\"))\\n(def ^:private chez-string->utf8 (scheme/proc \\\"string->utf8\\\"))\\n(def ^:private chez-bytevector-length (scheme/proc \\\"bytevector-length\\\"))\\n(def ^:private chez-bytevector-u8-ref (scheme/proc \\\"bytevector-u8-ref\\\"))\\n(def ^:private chez-string-normalize-nfc (scheme/proc \\\"string-normalize-nfc\\\"))\\n\\n\\n(defn- scalar-at\\n  [s i]\\n  (chez-char->integer (chez-string-ref s i)))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; Code units\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(defn unit-count\\n  \\\"P. The number of UTF-16 code units of string `s`: one per code point,\\n   two for a code point above U+FFFF.\\\"\\n  [s]\\n  (let [n (chez-string-length s)]\\n    (loop [i 0\\n           units 0]\\n      (if (< i n)\\n        (recur (inc i) (if (< (scalar-at s i) 0x10000) (inc units) (+ units 2)))\\n        units))))\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private code-unit-hint\\n  \\\"Where the last `code-unit` call stopped: `[s ci at]`, scalar `ci` of\\n   `s` starts at code unit `at`. A Chez string is indexed by scalar, so\\n   finding unit `i` means walking the scalars before it; a call on the\\n   same (identical) string at a unit index at or past the hint resumes\\n   there instead of at 0, which makes an ascending walk over the units\\n   (every caller's shape, e.g. yin.vm.debruijn's) linear in the string\\n   rather than quadratic. Any other call starts at 0, as without the\\n   hint, and answers the same. The hint is one immutable vector swapped\\n   whole, so a racing thread at worst restarts from 0; it keeps the last\\n   such string reachable until the next call. The resume is sound only\\n   while `s` is never mutated between calls: a Chez string is mutable,\\n   and nothing under src/ mutates one (no `string-set!`).\\\"\\n  (atom nil))\\n\\n\\n(defn code-unit\\n  \\\"P. The UTF-16 code unit at index `i` of `s`, 0 to 0xFFFF; the caller\\n   has checked 0 <= i < `unit-count`. A code point above U+FFFF answers\\n   its high surrogate at its first unit and its low at its second.\\\"\\n  [s i]\\n  (let [[hs hci hat] @code-unit-hint\\n        resume? (and (identical? hs s) (<= hat i))]\\n    (loop [ci (if resume? hci 0)\\n           at (if resume? hat 0)]\\n      (let [cp (scalar-at s ci)]\\n        (if (< cp 0x10000)\\n          (if (= at i)\\n            (do (reset! code-unit-hint [s ci at]) cp)\\n            (recur (inc ci) (inc at)))\\n          (cond (= at i) (do (reset! code-unit-hint [s ci at])\\n                             (+ 0xD800 (quot (- cp 0x10000) 0x400)))\\n                (= (inc at) i) (do (reset! code-unit-hint [s ci at])\\n                                   (+ 0xDC00 (rem (- cp 0x10000) 0x400)))\\n                :else (recur (inc ci) (+ at 2))))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- code-units\\n  \\\"The UTF-16 code units of `s` as a vector, in one pass over its\\n   scalars: what `code-unit` answers at each index 0 to `unit-count`.\\\"\\n  [s]\\n  (let [n (chez-string-length s)]\\n    (loop [ci 0\\n           out (transient [])]\\n      (if (< ci n)\\n        (let [cp (scalar-at s ci)]\\n          (recur (inc ci)\\n                 (if (< cp 0x10000)\\n                   (conj! out cp)\\n                   (-> out\\n                       (conj! (+ 0xD800 (quot (- cp 0x10000) 0x400)))\\n                       (conj! (+ 0xDC00 (rem (- cp 0x10000) 0x400)))))))\\n        (persistent! out)))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- high-surrogate?\\n  [u]\\n  (<= 0xD800 u 0xDBFF))\\n\\n\\n(defn- low-surrogate?\\n  [u]\\n  (<= 0xDC00 u 0xDFFF))\\n\\n\\n(defn units->string\\n  \\\"P. The host string of the UTF-16 code units `units` (each 0 to\\n   0xFFFF). A surrogate pair becomes its one code point. An unpaired\\n   surrogate has no Chez string: unimplemented on jolt (see the namespace\\n   docstring), thrown as `:jolt/unimplemented`, not as a profile refusal.\\n   An integer unit outside 0 to 0xFFFF is out of the domain; it throws\\n   what the JVM's `(char u)` throws, an IllegalArgumentException\\n   \\\\\\\"Value out of range for char: u\\\\\\\" with no ex-data, for the first such\\n   unit in order and before anything else is looked at (the JVM reaches\\n   it even past an unpaired surrogate, which it can hold), where Chez's\\n   `integer->char` would silently build the scalar above 0xFFFF (review\\n   J1-R5).\\\"\\n  [units]\\n  (let [units (vec units)\\n        n (count units)]\\n    (doseq [u units]\\n      (when (and (integer? u) (not (<= 0 u 0xFFFF)))\\n        (throw (IllegalArgumentException. (str \\\"Value out of range for char: \\\" u)))))\\n    (loop [i 0\\n           out (transient [])]\\n      (if (< i n)\\n        (let [u (nth units i)\\n              v (when (< (inc i) n) (nth units (inc i)))]\\n          (cond\\n            (and (high-surrogate? u) v (low-surrogate? v))\\n            (recur (+ i 2)\\n                   (conj! out (chez-integer->char (+ 0x10000\\n                                                     (* (- u 0xD800) 0x400)\\n                                                     (- v 0xDC00)))))\\n            (or (high-surrogate? u) (low-surrogate? u))\\n            (throw (ex-info (str \\\"jolt: units->string cannot build a string holding the unpaired surrogate \\\"\\n                                 u \\\" at unit \\\" i \\\": a Chez string holds only Unicode scalar values\\\")\\n                            {:jolt/unimplemented 'dao.host.text/units->string\\n                             :unit u\\n                             :index i}))\\n            :else (recur (inc i) (conj! out (chez-integer->char u)))))\\n        (apply str (persistent! out))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn code-points\\n  \\\"P, table B. The code points of string `s` as a vector: a surrogate\\n   pair decodes to one code point, an unpaired surrogate is its own.\\n   Over a Chez string that is exactly its scalars, read in one pass: a\\n   scalar below U+10000 is one unit and never a surrogate (Chez holds\\n   none), so it decodes to itself; a scalar above is a high-low pair,\\n   which decodes back to it; no unpaired surrogate is left to emit.\\\"\\n  [s]\\n  (let [n (chez-string-length s)]\\n    (loop [ci 0\\n           out (transient [])]\\n      (if (< ci n)\\n        (recur (inc ci) (conj! out (scalar-at s ci)))\\n        (persistent! out)))))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; UTF-8\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(defn- check-surrogates!\\n  \\\"Refuse `:unpaired-surrogate` unless every surrogate of `s` is paired,\\n   over its code units built once (`code-units`).\\\"\\n  [entry s]\\n  (let [units (code-units s)\\n        n (count units)]\\n    (loop [i 0]\\n      (when (< i n)\\n        (let [c (nth units i)]\\n          (cond\\n            (high-surrogate? c)\\n            (if (and (< (inc i) n) (low-surrogate? (nth units (inc i))))\\n              (recur (+ i 2))\\n              (refuse entry :unpaired-surrogate (str \\\"unpaired high surrogate at \\\" i)))\\n            (low-surrogate? c)\\n            (refuse entry :unpaired-surrogate (str \\\"unpaired low surrogate at \\\" i))\\n            :else (recur (inc i))))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn utf8-encode\\n  \\\"P, fresh. The UTF-8 bytes of string `s`, as a jolt byte array (Chez's\\n   `string->utf8`, copied out of its bytevector). A string holding an\\n   unpaired surrogate is refused `:unpaired-surrogate` before any host\\n   encoder sees it; on jolt no such string exists.\\\"\\n  [s]\\n26:                :jolt [[yang.python.antlr.parser :as python.antlr.parser]\\n151:                              :jolt \\\"--codec takes cbor, not \\\"\\n689:     :jolt (do (flush) (System/exit 1))\\n753:     #?(:cljd false :jolt true :default false)\\n757:            #?(:jolt (if (python.antlr.parser/available?)\\n759:                            \\\" (native/jolt-parser), in process; no process\\\"\\n761:                       \\\"python: unavailable-parser (the native parser library is missing or incompatible; bb build:jolt-native)\\\")\\n772:     :jolt (do (flush) (System/exit exit))\\n1079:  \\\"Serial shell owner shared by the JVM and jolt. Host policy supplies\\n1108:   :jolt nil\\n1204:#?(:jolt\\n1253:       \\\"The engine's own jolt entry point: the `mem` and `file:<dir>` stores\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n37:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_17\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"mkdir -p build/p1c-r3\\nsed -n '1,100p' ../datomworld/src/cljc/dao/host/text.cljc\\nrg -n 'defn nfc|Normalizer|icu4j' ../datomworld/{deps.edn,src/cljc/dao/host/text.cljc}\\nrg -n 'jolt-toolchain|GAMBIT_PREFIX|CHEZ=' build/p1c-r2/matrix.log | head -25\\nsed -n '1,200p' host/gambit/source-loader-test.ss\\nsed -n '20,65p' ../datomworld/src/cljc/{dao/space/store.cljc,yin/repl/frontends.cljc}\\nrg -n ':jolt|runtime-features' host/chez/reader.ss host/gambit/boot.ss | tail -20\\nrg -n '\\\"'^gambit|''^jolt'\\\"' ../datomworld/docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-gambit-ffi-stage2.md | head\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"(ns dao.host.text\\n  \\\"The host strings of the primitive profile\\n   (docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md §4.2, table A). A host string\\n   is a sequence of UTF-16 code units on every host (JVM `String`, JS\\n   string, Dart `String`).\\n\\n   Table A, kind P (§2.4): `unit-count`, `code-unit`, `units->string`,\\n   `utf8-encode`, `utf8-decode`, `nfc`. Equal arguments give equal\\n   results on every host:\\n   - `utf8-encode` refuses an unpaired surrogate `:unpaired-surrogate`\\n     instead of substituting (the JVM's `?`, U+FFFD elsewhere), so the\\n     bytes of every string it accepts are identical on every host; it\\n     returns fresh bytes the caller holds the mutation right to.\\n   - `utf8-decode` is strict RFC 3629: an overlong form, an encoded\\n     surrogate, a code point above U+10FFFF or a truncated sequence is\\n     refused `:invalid-utf8`, never decoded to U+FFFD; a leading BOM is\\n     content. It is the decoder of `dao.jing.cbor` (`utf8`), extracted.\\n   - `nfc` is Unicode Normalization Form C (`java.text.Normalizer`,\\n     `String.prototype.normalize`, the `unorm_dart` package); the profile\\n     pins Unicode 16.0 (§2.5).\\n\\n   Table B, P, no host arm: `code-points`, over `unit-count` and\\n   `code-unit` (the private `yin.vm.data` helper, made public here).\\n\\n   Oracle: `dao.host.text-test` and the text rows of table D, re-asserted\\n   by `dao.host.conformance-test`. A refusal throws `ex-info` whose data\\n   carries `:dao.host/refusal`, a class keyword, and `:dao.host/entry`.\\n   Every reader conditional names `:cljd` first, because the ClojureDart\\n   build also reads `:clj`.\\\"\\n  #?@(:cljd [(:require [\\\"dart:convert\\\" :as convert]\\n                       [\\\"package:unorm_dart/unorm_dart.dart\\\" :as unorm])\\n             (:import [\\\"dart:core\\\" FormatException String]\\n                      [\\\"dart:typed_data\\\" Uint8List])]\\n      :clj [(:import (java.nio ByteBuffer)\\n                     (java.nio.charset CharacterCodingException CodingErrorAction\\n                                       StandardCharsets))]))\\n\\n\\n(defn- refuse\\n  [entry cls detail]\\n  (throw (ex-info (str \\\"dao.host refused: \\\" entry \\\" \\\" (name cls) \\\" (\\\" detail \\\")\\\")\\n                  {:dao.host/refusal cls :dao.host/entry entry :detail detail})))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; Code units\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(defn unit-count\\n  \\\"P. The number of UTF-16 code units of string `s`.\\\"\\n  [s]\\n  #?(:cljd (.-length ^String s)\\n     :clj (.length ^String s)\\n     :cljs (.-length s)))\\n\\n\\n(defn code-unit\\n  \\\"P. The UTF-16 code unit at index `i` of `s`, 0 to 0xFFFF; the caller\\n   has checked 0 <= i < `unit-count`.\\\"\\n  [s i]\\n  #?(:cljd (.codeUnitAt ^String s i)\\n     :clj (int (.charAt ^String s (int i)))\\n     :cljs (.charCodeAt s i)))\\n\\n\\n(defn units->string\\n  \\\"P. The host string of the UTF-16 code units `units` (each 0 to\\n   0xFFFF), unpaired surrogates included.\\\"\\n  [units]\\n  #?(:cljd (let [sb (StringBuffer)]\\n             (doseq [u units] (.writeCharCode sb u))\\n             (.toString sb))\\n     :clj (let [sb (StringBuilder.)]\\n            (doseq [u units] (.append sb (char u)))\\n            (.toString sb))\\n     :cljs (let [a (array)]\\n             (doseq [u units] (.push a (.fromCharCode js/String u)))\\n             (.join a \\\"\\\"))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- high-surrogate?\\n  [u]\\n  (<= 0xD800 u 0xDBFF))\\n\\n\\n(defn- low-surrogate?\\n  [u]\\n  (<= 0xDC00 u 0xDFFF))\\n\\n\\n(defn code-points\\n  \\\"P, table B. The code points of string `s` as a vector: a surrogate\\n   pair decodes to one code point, an unpaired surrogate is its own.\\\"\\n  [s]\\n  (let [n (unit-count s)]\\n    (loop [i 0\\n           out (transient [])]\\n      (if (< i n)\\n        (let [u (code-unit s i)\\n              v (when (< (inc i) n) (code-unit s (inc i)))]\\n../datomworld/src/cljc/dao/host/text.cljc:18:   - `nfc` is Unicode Normalization Form C (`java.text.Normalizer`,\\n../datomworld/src/cljc/dao/host/text.cljc:195:(defn nfc\\n../datomworld/src/cljc/dao/host/text.cljc:203:     :clj (java.text.Normalizer/normalize ^String s java.text.Normalizer$Form/NFC)\\n#!/usr/bin/env gsi-script\\n(##include \\\"boot.ss\\\")\\n(##include \\\"host-apply.ss\\\")\\n(##include \\\"source-loader.ss\\\")\\n\\n(define (source-check description expected run)\\n  (let ((got (with-exception-catcher\\n               (lambda (e) (jolt-throwable-message (jolt-unwrap-throw e)))\\n               run)))\\n    (unless (equal? expected got)\\n      (fprintf (current-error-port) \\\"gambitsourceload: FAIL ~a: expected ~s, got ~s\\\\n\\\"\\n               description expected got)\\n      (exit 1))))\\n\\n(define (ce src) (jolt-pr-readable (jolt-eval-string src)))\\n\\n(define (run-byte-rows suite)\\n  (let ((rows (call-with-input-file \\\"test/gambit/unit.edn\\\"\\n                (lambda (port) (jolt-read-string (gambit-read-all port))))))\\n    (for-each\\n      (lambda (row)\\n        (when (eq? (jolt-get row (keyword #f \\\"suite\\\")) suite)\\n          (let ((label (jolt-get row (keyword #f \\\"label\\\")))\\n                (source (jolt-get row (keyword #f \\\"expr\\\")))\\n                (expected (jolt-get row (keyword #f \\\"expected\\\"))))\\n            (source-check label expected (lambda () (ce source)))\\n            (printf \\\"  ok     ~a\\\\n\\\" label))))\\n      (seq->list (jolt-seq rows)))))\\n\\n(define roots '((\\\"test/gambit/fixtures/source-load\\\" \\\".jolt\\\" \\\".cljc\\\")))\\n(define args (cdr (command-line)))\\n(gambit-install-source-loader!\\n  (if (null? args)\\n      roots\\n      (cons (list (path-expand \\\"src/jolt\\\" (car args)) \\\".jolt\\\") roots))\\n  '(\\\"clojure.core\\\" \\\"jolt.ffi\\\"))\\n(source-check \\\"backend\\\" \\\":gambit\\\" (lambda () (ce \\\"(jolt.backend-scheme.session/target)\\\")))\\n(source-check \\\"loaded dependency and reader feature\\\" \\\"42\\\"\\n  (lambda () (ce \\\"(require '[p1c.source-entry :as entry]) (entry/result)\\\")))\\n(source-check \\\"caller namespace\\\" \\\"user\\\" chez-current-ns)\\n(source-check \\\"reload refusal\\\" \\\"require option is unsupported on the gambit source loader: :reload\\\"\\n  (lambda () (ce \\\"(require 'p1c.source-entry :reload)\\\")))\\n(source-check \\\"alias-only missing source\\\" \\\"No source for namespace: p1c.absent\\\"\\n  (lambda () (ce \\\"(require '[p1c.absent :as-alias absent]) (require 'p1c.absent)\\\")))\\n(source-check \\\"surrogate pair str apply\\\" \\\"\\\\\\\"😀\\\\\\\"\\\"\\n  (lambda () (ce \\\"(apply str [(char 55357) (char 56832)])\\\")))\\n(source-check \\\"long source literal through compiler\\\" \\\"9000\\\"\\n  (lambda () (ce (string-append \\\"(count \\\\\\\"\\\" (make-string 9000 #\\\\x) \\\"\\\\\\\")\\\"))))\\n(run-byte-rows (keyword #f \\\"core-bytes\\\"))\\n(when (pair? args)\\n  (source-check \\\"load upstream dao.host.bytes\\\" \\\"nil\\\"\\n    (lambda () (ce \\\"(require 'dao.host.bytes)\\\")))\\n  (run-byte-rows (keyword #f \\\"dao-host-bytes\\\"))\\n  (display \\\"gambitdaobytes: PASS — upstream Dao byte arrays on native gsi\\\\n\\\"))\\n(display \\\"gambitsourceload: PASS — native source loading and compiler string emission\\\\n\\\")\\n   and `dht:<dir>` stores (yin.repl.store).\\\"\\n  (:require #?@(:cljd [[\\\"dart:io\\\" :as dart-io]\\n                       [clojure.edn :as edn]])\\n            #?(:cljd nil\\n               :clj [clojure.edn :as edn]\\n               :cljs [cljs.reader :as reader])\\n            [clojure.string :as str]\\n            [dao.jing :as jing]\\n            [dao.jing.file :as jing.file]\\n            [dao.space.index :as dao.index]\\n            [dao.space.store.fs :as fs]))\\n\\n\\n(def content-name\\n  \\\"The content log's own file name inside a durable store directory.\\\"\\n  \\\"content.jing\\\")\\n\\n\\n(def head-name\\n  \\\"The durable root pointer's own file name inside a durable store\\n   directory: the versioned record naming the latest published\\n   manifest.\\\"\\n  \\\"HEAD\\\")\\n\\n\\n(def head-version\\n  \\\"The HEAD record version this store writes and accepts.\\\"\\n  1)\\n\\n\\n(def unsupported-host-text\\n  \\\"The refusal for a durable directory on a host build that cannot open\\n   files.  Refusing is the contract: a build without a filesystem is never\\n   answered with an in-memory store its caller did not choose.\\\"\\n  (str \\\"a durable store directory is not supported on this host: opening \\\"\\n       \\\"the content log needs a filesystem, and this build has none; use \\\"\\n       \\\"an in-memory store, or run on a host with file support (the JVM, \\\"\\n       \\\"Node, or Dart)\\\"))\\n\\n\\n(defn host-file-support\\n  \\\"True when this build can open a file-backed store: the JVM, Node, and\\n   Dart can; a CLJS build without `js/require` cannot.\\\"\\n  []\\n  #?(:cljd true\\n     :cljs (exists? js/require)\\nhost/chez/reader.ss:12:;;   sets     -> {:jolt/type :jolt/set :value [...]}        (a FORM, not a set)\\nhost/chez/reader.ss:13:;;   #tag frm -> {:jolt/type :jolt/tagged :tag :#tag :form ...}  (NO data reader)\\nhost/chez/reader.ss:14:;;   #\\\"src\\\"   -> {:jolt/type :jolt/tagged :tag :regex :form \\\"src\\\"}\\nhost/chez/reader.ss:846:;; plus position, as flat :jolt.error/* keys. Three things follow from that.\\nhost/chez/reader.ss:1106:;; reader conditionals: jolt's feature set is {:jolt :clj :default}; the FIRST\\nhost/chez/reader.ss:1111:;; :jolt branch placed before :clj.\\nhost/chez/reader.ss:1799:;; read-string/read return DATA, so set literal FORMS ({:jolt/type :jolt/set\\n6:gambit-ffi-plan.gpt-6-astra.findings.md §2 Callbacks, §3 gate table), the codex\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9f2c1e4a-7b3d-4e8f-a6c5-d0e9f8a1b2c3","uuid":"01352f98-1803-4789-96b4-a5ba3a78a23a","timestamp":"2026-11-26T15:29:58.848Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"25:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_11\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,245p' ../datomworld/src/jolt/dao/host/hash.jolt; sed -n '1,220p' ../datomworld/src/jolt/dao/host/text.jolt; rg -n 'jolt|Gambit' ../datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/main.cljc | tail -35\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"(ns dao.host.hash\\n  \\\"The jolt override of src/cljc/dao/host/hash.cljc (the :jolt alias puts\\n   src/jolt first, docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md §4.2, §6.2,\\n   §6.3): the same public entries, arities, docstrings and refusal data\\n   (docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md §4.2, table A).\\n\\n   P entries (§2.4): `blake3-bytes` and `sha256-bytes`. They take host\\n   bytes (jolt's byte array) and return fresh host bytes representing the\\n   32-byte digest (256 bits). They leave their arguments unmodified.\\n\\n   Both are `:blocking` calls into native/jolt-crypto, which `bb\\n   build:jolt-native` builds: BLAKE3 is the vendored upstream C reference,\\n   SHA-256 the declared system libcrypto's EVP API. The input is copied into\\n   caller-owned native memory (never a C string, so embedded NUL is data),\\n   the digest is read back into a fresh jolt byte array, and the memory is\\n   freed on every path: each native acquisition owns its own release,\\n   established as soon as it is acquired (`with-native`), so a later\\n   allocation or copy that fails still frees every earlier buffer, and each\\n   free runs whether or not another failed.\\n\\n   Loading this namespace is the provenance gate (§6.3): it refuses, by\\n   throwing, unless the wrapper writes this namespace's layout and reports\\n   that every EVP entry resolved to exactly the library jolt-toolchain.edn\\n   qualified for this platform (:native :libcrypto :qualified): the same\\n   canonical real path (which the declared :path must also resolve to), the\\n   same OpenSSL_version_num and the same file SHA-256, at no lower than\\n   :min-version. An upgrade, a repointed link or a replaced file therefore\\n   fails until requalified (the procedure is in jolt-toolchain.edn). No\\n   digest is computed before that check passes; a missing library fails the\\n   load.\\n\\n   String digesting is a table B portable composition over `dao.host.text`\\n   and these byte primitives (`blake3`, `sha256`, `digest-string`,\\n   `digest-bytes`), hosted in `dao.jing`.\\\"\\n  (:require\\n    [clojure.edn :as edn]\\n    [clojure.string :as str]\\n    [dao.host.bytes :as bytes]\\n    [jolt.ffi :as ffi]))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; The native library and its provenance\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(def ^:private platform\\n  ;; as yang.antlr.cst decides it: jolt's os.name/os.arch shim can answer\\n  ;; nil, which reads as Linux and x86_64\\n  (let [os (str/lower-case (or (System/getProperty \\\"os.name\\\") \\\"\\\"))\\n        arch (System/getProperty \\\"os.arch\\\")]\\n    (str (if (str/includes? os \\\"mac\\\") \\\"darwin\\\" \\\"linux\\\") \\\"-\\\"\\n         (if (#{\\\"aarch64\\\" \\\"arm64\\\"} arch) \\\"arm64\\\" \\\"x86_64\\\"))))\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private library\\n  ;; the wrapper `bb build:jolt-native` builds, under the working directory\\n  (str (System/getProperty \\\"user.dir\\\") \\\"/build/native/\\\" platform\\n       \\\"/jolt-crypto/lib/libjolt_crypto\\\"\\n       (if (str/starts-with? platform \\\"darwin\\\") \\\".dylib\\\" \\\".so\\\")))\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private libcrypto-declaration\\n  (get-in (edn/read-string (slurp (str (System/getProperty \\\"user.dir\\\")\\n                                       \\\"/jolt-toolchain.edn\\\")))\\n          [:native :libcrypto]))\\n\\n\\n(ffi/load-library library)\\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-layout-version \\\"jolt_crypto_layout_version\\\" [] :int)\\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-provenance \\\"jolt_crypto_provenance\\\" [:pointer :size_t] :int)\\n\\n\\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-blake3 \\\"jolt_crypto_blake3\\\"\\n            [:pointer :size_t :pointer] :int :blocking)\\n\\n\\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-sha256 \\\"jolt_crypto_sha256\\\"\\n            [:pointer :size_t :pointer] :int :blocking)\\n\\n\\n;; 3 (J2): the random entry joined the exported set (jolt_crypto_random for\\n;; yin.vm.linker.sign/generate's :jolt arm); a J1b-built wrapper answers 2 and\\n;; is refused here rather than at a missing-symbol error in the linker.\\n;; 4 (J6c): jolt_crypto_sha1 joined it (dao.stream.ws.crypto) and EVP_sha1\\n;; the provenance audit; a J2-built wrapper answers 3 and is refused here.\\n(def ^:private layout 4)\\n\\n\\n(defn- refuse-load\\n  [message data]\\n  (throw (ex-info (str \\\"jolt-crypto refused: \\\" message)\\n                  (assoc data :library library))))\\n\\n\\n(when-not (= layout (c-layout-version))\\n  (refuse-load \\\"the library writes another layout\\\"\\n               {:expected layout, :actual (c-layout-version)}))\\n\\n\\n(defn- with-native\\n  \\\"(f p) over caller-owned native memory `p` of `n` bytes (one for an empty\\n   input), freed however `f` exits. The release is established before\\n   anything else can fail, so nesting one call per acquisition frees each\\n   buffer independently of the others.\\\"\\n  [n f]\\n  (let [p (ffi/alloc (max 1 n))]\\n    (try\\n      (when (ffi/null? p)\\n        (throw (ex-info \\\"jolt-crypto native allocation failed\\\" {:bytes n})))\\n      (f p)\\n      (finally (ffi/free p)))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- version-number\\n  \\\"OpenSSL_version_num of a \\\\\\\"major.minor.patch\\\\\\\" text (OpenSSL 3's\\n   0xMNN00PP0 form).\\\"\\n  [text]\\n  (let [[major minor patch] (map #(Long/parseLong %) (str/split text #\\\"\\\\.\\\"))]\\n    (+ (bit-shift-left major 28) (bit-shift-left minor 20) (bit-shift-left patch 4))))\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private provenance\\n  (with-native 4096\\n    (fn [buf]\\n      (let [n (c-provenance buf 4096)]\\n        (when (neg? n)\\n          (refuse-load \\\"libcrypto provenance unavailable\\\" {:status n}))\\n        (let [[num text path digest] (str/split (ffi/read-bytes buf n) #\\\"\\\\t\\\")]\\n          {:version-num (Long/parseLong num), :version text, :path path,\\n           :sha256 digest})))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- canonical\\n  [path]\\n  (.getCanonicalPath (java.io.File. ^String path)))\\n\\n\\n(let [declared (get-in libcrypto-declaration [:path (keyword platform)])\\n      qualified (get-in libcrypto-declaration [:qualified (keyword platform)])\\n      minimum (:min-version libcrypto-declaration)\\n      refuse (fn [message]\\n               (refuse-load message {:declared declared, :qualified qualified,\\n                                     :provenance provenance}))]\\n  (when-not (and declared qualified minimum)\\n    (refuse \\\"jolt-toolchain.edn qualifies no libcrypto for this platform\\\"))\\n  ;; canonical paths: dyld answers the real path behind Homebrew's opt/\\n  ;; link, and a Linux loader may name the file through /lib\\n  (when-not (= (canonical (:path provenance)) (:canonical-path qualified))\\n    (refuse \\\"EVP resolved to an undeclared libcrypto (canonical path differs from the qualified one)\\\"))\\n  (when-not (= (canonical declared) (:canonical-path qualified))\\n    (refuse \\\"the declared libcrypto path resolves to another file than the qualified one (repointed)\\\"))\\n  (when-not (= (:version-num provenance) (:version-num qualified))\\n    (refuse \\\"libcrypto version differs from the qualified one (upgrade or downgrade)\\\"))\\n  (when-not (= (:sha256 provenance) (:sha256 qualified))\\n    (refuse \\\"libcrypto file digest differs from the qualified one (replaced)\\\"))\\n  (when (< (:version-num provenance) (version-number minimum))\\n    (refuse \\\"libcrypto is older than the declared minimum\\\")))\\n\\n\\n(defn- digest\\n  \\\"The 32-byte result of wrapper entry `c-fn` over `bs`, copied into a fresh\\n   jolt byte array.\\\"\\n  [entry c-fn bs]\\n  (let [n (alength bs)]\\n    (with-native n\\n      (fn [in]\\n        (when (pos? n) (ffi/write-array in bs))\\n        (with-native 32\\n          (fn [out]\\n            (let [status (c-fn in n out)]\\n              (when-not (zero? status)\\n                (throw (ex-info (str \\\"jolt-crypto \\\" entry \\\" failed\\\")\\n                                {:entry entry, :status status})))\\n              (ffi/read-array out 32))))))))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; The profile surface\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(defn- check-bytes!\\n  [entry bs]\\n  (when-not (bytes/bytes? bs)\\n    (throw (ex-info (str \\\"dao.host refused: \\\" entry \\\" unsupported-value (expected host bytes)\\\")\\n                    {:dao.host/refusal :unsupported-value\\n                     :dao.host/entry entry}))))\\n\\n\\n(defn blake3-bytes\\n  \\\"P. 32-byte BLAKE3 digest of host bytes `bs`, returning fresh host bytes.\\\"\\n  [bs]\\n  (check-bytes! 'blake3-bytes bs)\\n  (digest 'blake3-bytes c-blake3 bs))\\n\\n\\n(defn sha256-bytes\\n  \\\"P. 32-byte SHA-256 digest of host bytes `bs`, returning fresh host bytes.\\\"\\n  [bs]\\n  (check-bytes! 'sha256-bytes bs)\\n  (digest 'sha256-bytes c-sha256 bs))\\n(ns dao.host.text\\n  \\\"The jolt override of src/cljc/dao/host/text.cljc (the :jolt alias puts\\n   src/jolt first, docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md §4.2): the\\n   same public entries, arities and refusal data\\n   (docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md §4.2, table A).\\n\\n   A jolt string is a Chez string: a sequence of Unicode scalar values\\n   (code points), not of UTF-16 code units; `(count \\\\\\\"😀\\\\\\\")` is 1, and no\\n   Chez string can hold a surrogate code point (V: `integer->char` refuses\\n   #xD800). The code-unit entries are therefore computed over the UTF-16\\n   view of the scalar values: `unit-count` counts a code point above\\n   U+FFFF as two units and `code-unit` answers its high or low surrogate.\\n   Exactly the strings with every surrogate paired are representable, so:\\n\\n   - `units->string` builds every string the profile admits EXCEPT one\\n     holding an unpaired surrogate, which a Chez string cannot hold. It\\n     does not invent a value for that input: it throws an ex-info whose\\n     data carries `:jolt/unimplemented` (and no `:dao.host/refusal`). J1\\n     stopped on that entry; the options are in the J1 report.\\n   - `utf8-encode`'s unpaired-surrogate refusal can never be reached on\\n     jolt, because no jolt string has an unpaired surrogate; the check is\\n     kept, verbatim, over the code-unit view.\\n   - `utf8-decode` is a strict RFC 3629 decoder written here over the\\n     bytes (Unicode table 3-7), never Chez's `utf8->string`, which\\n     substitutes; a leading BOM is content.\\n   - `nfc` is Chez's `string-normalize-nfc`. Chez 10.4 implements Unicode\\n     17.0.0 (release notes, \\\\\\\"Unicode 17.0.0 support (10.4.0)\\\\\\\"), not the\\n     profile's pinned 16.0; by the Unicode normalization stability policy\\n     the two agree on every string of 16.0-assigned code points, and may\\n     differ only on code points first assigned in 17.0.\\\"\\n  (:require\\n    [jolt.scheme :as scheme]))\\n\\n\\n(defn- refuse\\n  [entry cls detail]\\n  (throw (ex-info (str \\\"dao.host refused: \\\" entry \\\" \\\" (name cls) \\\" (\\\" detail \\\")\\\")\\n                  {:dao.host/refusal cls :dao.host/entry entry :detail detail})))\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private chez-string-length (scheme/proc \\\"string-length\\\"))\\n(def ^:private chez-string-ref (scheme/proc \\\"string-ref\\\"))\\n(def ^:private chez-char->integer (scheme/proc \\\"char->integer\\\"))\\n(def ^:private chez-integer->char (scheme/proc \\\"integer->char\\\"))\\n(def ^:private chez-string->utf8 (scheme/proc \\\"string->utf8\\\"))\\n(def ^:private chez-bytevector-length (scheme/proc \\\"bytevector-length\\\"))\\n(def ^:private chez-bytevector-u8-ref (scheme/proc \\\"bytevector-u8-ref\\\"))\\n(def ^:private chez-string-normalize-nfc (scheme/proc \\\"string-normalize-nfc\\\"))\\n\\n\\n(defn- scalar-at\\n  [s i]\\n  (chez-char->integer (chez-string-ref s i)))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; Code units\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(defn unit-count\\n  \\\"P. The number of UTF-16 code units of string `s`: one per code point,\\n   two for a code point above U+FFFF.\\\"\\n  [s]\\n  (let [n (chez-string-length s)]\\n    (loop [i 0\\n           units 0]\\n      (if (< i n)\\n        (recur (inc i) (if (< (scalar-at s i) 0x10000) (inc units) (+ units 2)))\\n        units))))\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private code-unit-hint\\n  \\\"Where the last `code-unit` call stopped: `[s ci at]`, scalar `ci` of\\n   `s` starts at code unit `at`. A Chez string is indexed by scalar, so\\n   finding unit `i` means walking the scalars before it; a call on the\\n   same (identical) string at a unit index at or past the hint resumes\\n   there instead of at 0, which makes an ascending walk over the units\\n   (every caller's shape, e.g. yin.vm.debruijn's) linear in the string\\n   rather than quadratic. Any other call starts at 0, as without the\\n   hint, and answers the same. The hint is one immutable vector swapped\\n   whole, so a racing thread at worst restarts from 0; it keeps the last\\n   such string reachable until the next call. The resume is sound only\\n   while `s` is never mutated between calls: a Chez string is mutable,\\n   and nothing under src/ mutates one (no `string-set!`).\\\"\\n  (atom nil))\\n\\n\\n(defn code-unit\\n  \\\"P. The UTF-16 code unit at index `i` of `s`, 0 to 0xFFFF; the caller\\n   has checked 0 <= i < `unit-count`. A code point above U+FFFF answers\\n   its high surrogate at its first unit and its low at its second.\\\"\\n  [s i]\\n  (let [[hs hci hat] @code-unit-hint\\n        resume? (and (identical? hs s) (<= hat i))]\\n    (loop [ci (if resume? hci 0)\\n           at (if resume? hat 0)]\\n      (let [cp (scalar-at s ci)]\\n        (if (< cp 0x10000)\\n          (if (= at i)\\n            (do (reset! code-unit-hint [s ci at]) cp)\\n            (recur (inc ci) (inc at)))\\n          (cond (= at i) (do (reset! code-unit-hint [s ci at])\\n                             (+ 0xD800 (quot (- cp 0x10000) 0x400)))\\n                (= (inc at) i) (do (reset! code-unit-hint [s ci at])\\n                                   (+ 0xDC00 (rem (- cp 0x10000) 0x400)))\\n                :else (recur (inc ci) (+ at 2))))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- code-units\\n  \\\"The UTF-16 code units of `s` as a vector, in one pass over its\\n   scalars: what `code-unit` answers at each index 0 to `unit-count`.\\\"\\n  [s]\\n  (let [n (chez-string-length s)]\\n    (loop [ci 0\\n           out (transient [])]\\n      (if (< ci n)\\n        (let [cp (scalar-at s ci)]\\n          (recur (inc ci)\\n                 (if (< cp 0x10000)\\n                   (conj! out cp)\\n                   (-> out\\n                       (conj! (+ 0xD800 (quot (- cp 0x10000) 0x400)))\\n                       (conj! (+ 0xDC00 (rem (- cp 0x10000) 0x400)))))))\\n        (persistent! out)))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- high-surrogate?\\n  [u]\\n  (<= 0xD800 u 0xDBFF))\\n\\n\\n(defn- low-surrogate?\\n  [u]\\n  (<= 0xDC00 u 0xDFFF))\\n\\n\\n(defn units->string\\n  \\\"P. The host string of the UTF-16 code units `units` (each 0 to\\n   0xFFFF). A surrogate pair becomes its one code point. An unpaired\\n   surrogate has no Chez string: unimplemented on jolt (see the namespace\\n   docstring), thrown as `:jolt/unimplemented`, not as a profile refusal.\\n   An integer unit outside 0 to 0xFFFF is out of the domain; it throws\\n   what the JVM's `(char u)` throws, an IllegalArgumentException\\n   \\\\\\\"Value out of range for char: u\\\\\\\" with no ex-data, for the first such\\n   unit in order and before anything else is looked at (the JVM reaches\\n   it even past an unpaired surrogate, which it can hold), where Chez's\\n   `integer->char` would silently build the scalar above 0xFFFF (review\\n   J1-R5).\\\"\\n  [units]\\n  (let [units (vec units)\\n        n (count units)]\\n    (doseq [u units]\\n      (when (and (integer? u) (not (<= 0 u 0xFFFF)))\\n        (throw (IllegalArgumentException. (str \\\"Value out of range for char: \\\" u)))))\\n    (loop [i 0\\n           out (transient [])]\\n      (if (< i n)\\n        (let [u (nth units i)\\n              v (when (< (inc i) n) (nth units (inc i)))]\\n          (cond\\n            (and (high-surrogate? u) v (low-surrogate? v))\\n            (recur (+ i 2)\\n                   (conj! out (chez-integer->char (+ 0x10000\\n                                                     (* (- u 0xD800) 0x400)\\n                                                     (- v 0xDC00)))))\\n            (or (high-surrogate? u) (low-surrogate? u))\\n            (throw (ex-info (str \\\"jolt: units->string cannot build a string holding the unpaired surrogate \\\"\\n                                 u \\\" at unit \\\" i \\\": a Chez string holds only Unicode scalar values\\\")\\n                            {:jolt/unimplemented 'dao.host.text/units->string\\n                             :unit u\\n                             :index i}))\\n            :else (recur (inc i) (conj! out (chez-integer->char u)))))\\n        (apply str (persistent! out))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn code-points\\n  \\\"P, table B. The code points of string `s` as a vector: a surrogate\\n   pair decodes to one code point, an unpaired surrogate is its own.\\n   Over a Chez string that is exactly its scalars, read in one pass: a\\n   scalar below U+10000 is one unit and never a surrogate (Chez holds\\n   none), so it decodes to itself; a scalar above is a high-low pair,\\n   which decodes back to it; no unpaired surrogate is left to emit.\\\"\\n  [s]\\n  (let [n (chez-string-length s)]\\n    (loop [ci 0\\n           out (transient [])]\\n      (if (< ci n)\\n        (recur (inc ci) (conj! out (scalar-at s ci)))\\n        (persistent! out)))))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; UTF-8\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(defn- check-surrogates!\\n  \\\"Refuse `:unpaired-surrogate` unless every surrogate of `s` is paired,\\n   over its code units built once (`code-units`).\\\"\\n  [entry s]\\n  (let [units (code-units s)\\n        n (count units)]\\n    (loop [i 0]\\n      (when (< i n)\\n        (let [c (nth units i)]\\n          (cond\\n            (high-surrogate? c)\\n            (if (and (< (inc i) n) (low-surrogate? (nth units (inc i))))\\n              (recur (+ i 2))\\n              (refuse entry :unpaired-surrogate (str \\\"unpaired high surrogate at \\\" i)))\\n            (low-surrogate? c)\\n            (refuse entry :unpaired-surrogate (str \\\"unpaired low surrogate at \\\" i))\\n            :else (recur (inc i))))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn utf8-encode\\n  \\\"P, fresh. The UTF-8 bytes of string `s`, as a jolt byte array (Chez's\\n   `string->utf8`, copied out of its bytevector). A string holding an\\n   unpaired surrogate is refused `:unpaired-surrogate` before any host\\n   encoder sees it; on jolt no such string exists.\\\"\\n  [s]\\n26:                :jolt [[yang.python.antlr.parser :as python.antlr.parser]\\n151:                              :jolt \\\"--codec takes cbor, not \\\"\\n689:     :jolt (do (flush) (System/exit 1))\\n753:     #?(:cljd false :jolt true :default false)\\n757:            #?(:jolt (if (python.antlr.parser/available?)\\n759:                            \\\" (native/jolt-parser), in process; no process\\\"\\n761:                       \\\"python: unavailable-parser (the native parser library is missing or incompatible; bb build:jolt-native)\\\")\\n772:     :jolt (do (flush) (System/exit exit))\\n1079:  \\\"Serial shell owner shared by the JVM and jolt. Host policy supplies\\n1108:   :jolt nil\\n1204:#?(:jolt\\n1253:       \\\"The engine's own jolt entry point: the `mem` and `file:<dir>` stores\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n37:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_17\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"mkdir -p build/p1c-r3\\nsed -n '1,100p' ../datomworld/src/cljc/dao/host/text.cljc\\nrg -n 'defn nfc|Normalizer|icu4j' ../datomworld/{deps.edn,src/cljc/dao/host/text.cljc}\\nrg -n 'jolt-toolchain|GAMBIT_PREFIX|CHEZ=' build/p1c-r2/matrix.log | head -25\\nsed -n '1,200p' host/gambit/source-loader-test.ss\\nsed -n '20,65p' ../datomworld/src/cljc/{dao/space/store.cljc,yin/repl/frontends.cljc}\\nrg -n ':jolt|runtime-features' host/chez/reader.ss host/gambit/boot.ss | tail -20\\nrg -n '\\\"'^gambit|''^jolt'\\\"' ../datomworld/docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-gambit-ffi-stage2.md | head\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"(ns dao.host.text\\n  \\\"The host strings of the primitive profile\\n   (docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md §4.2, table A). A host string\\n   is a sequence of UTF-16 code units on every host (JVM `String`, JS\\n   string, Dart `String`).\\n\\n   Table A, kind P (§2.4): `unit-count`, `code-unit`, `units->string`,\\n   `utf8-encode`, `utf8-decode`, `nfc`. Equal arguments give equal\\n   results on every host:\\n   - `utf8-encode` refuses an unpaired surrogate `:unpaired-surrogate`\\n     instead of substituting (the JVM's `?`, U+FFFD elsewhere), so the\\n     bytes of every string it accepts are identical on every host; it\\n     returns fresh bytes the caller holds the mutation right to.\\n   - `utf8-decode` is strict RFC 3629: an overlong form, an encoded\\n     surrogate, a code point above U+10FFFF or a truncated sequence is\\n     refused `:invalid-utf8`, never decoded to U+FFFD; a leading BOM is\\n     content. It is the decoder of `dao.jing.cbor` (`utf8`), extracted.\\n   - `nfc` is Unicode Normalization Form C (`java.text.Normalizer`,\\n     `String.prototype.normalize`, the `unorm_dart` package); the profile\\n     pins Unicode 16.0 (§2.5).\\n\\n   Table B, P, no host arm: `code-points`, over `unit-count` and\\n   `code-unit` (the private `yin.vm.data` helper, made public here).\\n\\n   Oracle: `dao.host.text-test` and the text rows of table D, re-asserted\\n   by `dao.host.conformance-test`. A refusal throws `ex-info` whose data\\n   carries `:dao.host/refusal`, a class keyword, and `:dao.host/entry`.\\n   Every reader conditional names `:cljd` first, because the ClojureDart\\n   build also reads `:clj`.\\\"\\n  #?@(:cljd [(:require [\\\"dart:convert\\\" :as convert]\\n                       [\\\"package:unorm_dart/unorm_dart.dart\\\" :as unorm])\\n            