rminating → returns `\\` as a char. In Clojure, `\\\\` is the backslash character. ✓. But `\\;` → char `;`. ✓. Fine.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtle bug: `read-char-lit` for `\\newline` etc. uses `read-token` which terminates on whitespace and macro chars. `\\newline` reads token \"newline\". Good. `\\u1234` reads \"u1234\". Good. `\\o377` reads \"o377\". Good.\n\n**B. Symbol parsing edge cases.** `parse-symbol-token` handles `/` specially. But symbols like `foo/bar` (qualified) → namespace `foo`, name `bar`. And `/` alone → `'/'`. What about symbols ending in `/` or with trailing dots? Minor. Not part of the float-kind scope, and covered by yin.repl-test (which passes).\n\n**C. The `+`/`-` number detection vs symbols.** `read-form-1` line 649: `(and (or (= c 43) (= c 45)) (< (inc i) n) (digit? (char-at s (inc i))))`. So `-1` → number. But what about `-` followed by a digit in a SYMBOL like `-1foo`? read-token reads `-1foo` (no terminating char), then `numeric-token/materialize :clojure \"-1foo\"` → classify-clojure: none of the regexes match → `:invalid` → throws. In real Clojure, `-1foo` is... actually invalid (can't have a symbol that looks like a number). tools.reader would throw too. So this is fine (explicit refusal).\n\n**D. `##` symbolic values.** Line 518-520: `(= c 35) (read-token) ... (numeric-token/materialize :clojure (str \"##\" sym-tok))`. But what about `##` followed by something invalid like `##foo`? classify-clojure: `##foo` doesn't match any → `:invalid` → throws. Good (explicit refusal). And `##Inf`/`##-Inf`/`##NaN` handled. ✓. Note: `##-Inf` — read-token reads `-Inf` (since `-` is not terminating and `-Inf` has no terminator) → token `-Inf` → `##-Inf`. ✓.\n\n**E. Potential issue: the reader's `materialize :clojure` for a token like `1e` or `1.2.3` or `08`.** classify-clojure: `1e` — matches float regex `^[+-]?[0-9]+(?:\\.[0-9]*(?:[eE]...)?|[eE][+-]?[0-9]+)$`? `1e` — the regex requires `[eE][+-]?[0-9]+` after, so `1e` has no digits after `e` → no match. Integer regex `^[+-]?(0|[1-9][0-9]*)(N?)$` → `1e` no match. → invalid → throws. ✓. `1.2.3` → float regex: `[0-9]+\\.[0-9]*(?:[eE]...)?` — `1.2` then `.3` extra → no match → invalid → throws. ✓. `08` → octal regex `^[+-]?0[0-7]+N?$` → `08` has `8` not in [0-7] → no match. Integer regex `(0|[1-9][0-9]*)` → `08` not match (starts with 0 but not just \"0\"). → invalid → throws. ✓ (Clojure also refuses `08`).\n\nThese are all correct. The reader is solid.\n\n**F. `parse-ratio` for `4/2` → `2` (integer).** The docstring says \"matching JVM Clojure's reader for e.g. 4/2 -> 2, 0/2 -> 0\". classify-clojure ratio regex → `4/2` matches → ratio → parse-ratio reduces → rd=1 → demote → 2. ✓. But is that Clojure-correct? In Clojure, `(read-string \"4/2\")` → `2` (yes, Clojure reduces ratio with denominator 1 to integer). ✓.\n\n**G. One more subtle: `parse-decimal` with trailing zeros and negative.** The decimal handling strips leading zeros via regex `#\"^0+\"`. For `10.03M` → int-digits \"10\", frac \"03\", digits-raw \"1003\", trimmed \"1003\". mantissa = 1003, scale-shift = 2, exponent = -2. cbor/decimal(-2, 1003). For `1M` → int \"1\" frac \"\" digits \"1\" → decimal(0, 1). For `-2.5M` → neg, digits \"25\" → decimal(-1, -25). This matches BigDecimal semantics. Good enough (covered by gate1 tests).\n\nSo no correctness bugs found in the reader. The implementation is high quality.\n\nLet me now also verify the `gate5-parked-float-result-crosses-the-round` test more carefully — it's a complex continuation test. It passed on JVM and Node (green). It's `(when repl-route? ...)`, so JVM+Node only. Fine.\n\nLet me also double check the claim \"Dart: 63/63 tests passed\" from green-dart-gates.log, and the Dart `:forms-only` honest refusal.","signature":"24b46dbb-869d-4953-8cf8-77c8a25c68d5"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":623,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":140544,"output_tokens":0,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_ma